      "file_upload_id",
    ],
  },
  {
    "description": "Notion | Correct the text of a block that is already on a page. Pass the one property named after the block's own type — \`paragraph\`, \`bulleted_list_item\`, \`to_do\`, \`heading_1\` and so on — carrying the new \`rich_text\`. The new \`rich_text\` replaces the block's existing text entirely, so send the whole corrected line and not just the changed part. Notion only lets an existing block's text (and \`checked\`, on a \`to_do\`) be updated; a block cannot be turned into another type this way. Editing in place keeps the block's id, its position on the page and any comments anchored to it — appending a corrected copy and archiving the old one does not.
Send exactly one block-type property. It goes at the top level of the request body, beside \`archived\`, in the same shape API-patch-block-children uses for a new block: \`{"paragraph": {"rich_text": [{"text": {"content": "…"}}]}}\`. There is no wrapper object around it — an earlier version of this spec had one, and Notion answered every such call with a validation error naming each block type as undefined.
Returns:
A block object. Its content sits under a key named by its own \`type\` — a paragraph carries \`paragraph\`, a heading \`heading_1\`.
Fields: object: string, id: string, parent: object, type: string, has_children: boolean, created_time: string, last_edited_time: string, created_by: object, last_edited_by: object, archived: boolean, in_trash: boolean, request_id: string
Error Responses:
400: Bad request",
